{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"d576916647ca741a","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is a subnet?**|What is a subnet?]]","x":760,"y":1843,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"1a28ee0de1e4ad1c","type":"text","text":"A `subnet` is a smaller network inside a VPC.  \nYou divide a VPC into subnets to organize resources.  \nFor example, you may create:\n```\nPublic subnet\nPrivate subnet\n```\nA public subnet can be used for web servers.  \nA private subnet can be used for databases.  \n**Example VPC and subnet design**  \nYour VPC may use:\n```\n10.0.0.0/16\n```\nThen you divide it into smaller subnets:\n```\n10.0.1.0/24    Public subnet\n10.0.2.0/24    Private subnet\n```\nThe VPC is the big network.  \nThe subnets are smaller parts inside that network.  ","x":1160,"y":1843,"width":500,"height":585},
		{"id":"cfdef20f6a2aaf3f","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is a public subnet?**|What is a public subnet?]]","x":760,"y":2488,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"cf156ffcfa6bb330","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is a private subnet?**|What is a private subnet?]]","x":760,"y":2798,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"22736dfc0ad0c6b7","type":"text","text":"A `public subnet` is a subnet that can connect to the internet through an `Internet Gateway`.  \nYou usually put internet-facing resources here.  \nFor example:\n```\nWeb server\nLoad balancer\nBastion host\n```","x":1160,"y":2488,"width":500,"height":250},
		{"id":"1f940d34fcf519ed","type":"text","text":"A `private subnet` is a subnet that is not directly reachable from the public internet.  \nYou usually put internal resources here.  \nFor example:\n```\nDatabase\nPrivate backend service\nInternal application server\n```\nPrivate resources can still access the internet through a `NAT Gateway`, but the internet cannot directly access them.  ","x":1160,"y":2798,"width":500,"height":308},
		{"id":"9b892119c1a34ec1","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is a subnet?**|What is a subnet?]]","x":760,"y":3333,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"8bf3043ea62c9b25","type":"text","text":"A `subnet` is a smaller network inside a VPC.  \nYou use subnets to organize resources.  \nFor example:\n```\nPublic subnet for web servers\nPrivate subnet for databases\n```","x":1160,"y":3333,"width":500,"height":220},
		{"id":"cfca1460601dd7cd","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is subnet sizing?**|What is subnet sizing?]]","x":760,"y":3640,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"d66f137812ecd31b","type":"text","text":"`Subnet sizing` means choosing how large a subnet should be.  \nYou choose the subnet size by selecting a CIDR block.  \nFor example:\n```\n10.0.1.0/24\n```\nA `/24` subnet gives 256 IP addresses.  \nYou should choose subnet sizes carefully so you have enough IP addresses for your resources.  ","x":1160,"y":3640,"width":500,"height":259},
		{"id":"2c4acfd443b4d773","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is a private IP address?**|What is a private IP address?]]","x":-680,"y":3859,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"c560abf1a490387a","type":"text","text":"A `private IP address` is an internal IP address used inside a private network.  \nExample:\n```\n10.0.1.20\n```\nPrivate IP addresses are used for communication inside the VPC.  \nThey are not directly reachable from the public internet.  ","x":-280,"y":3859,"width":500,"height":234},
		{"id":"38ffb293912dc8dd","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is a public IP address?**|What is a public IP address?]]","x":-680,"y":4134,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"266ab06645cfc2e3","type":"text","text":"A `public IP address` is an IP address that can be used on the public internet.  \nIf an EC2 instance has a public IP address and the correct routing/security settings, people from the internet can reach it.  ","x":-280,"y":4134,"width":500,"height":120},
		{"id":"6ecc6b8f4d4b59f2","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is an Elastic IP address?**|What is an Elastic IP address?]]","x":-680,"y":4314,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"037d01cca7a7d3aa","type":"text","text":"An `Elastic IP address` is a static public IP address that you can allocate in AWS and attach to resources.  \nUnlike a normal public IP address, an Elastic IP can stay the same.  \nFor example, you can attach it to an EC2 instance or a NAT Gateway.\n<!--SR:!2026-07-30,1,230-->","x":-280,"y":4314,"width":500,"height":355},
		{"id":"83c20171ca489618","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is source IP?**|What is source IP?]]","x":-680,"y":4509,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"49e73946e2ba9f5b","type":"text","text":"`Source IP` is the IP address where traffic comes from.  \nFor example:\n```\nUser IP → Web server\n```\nThe user's IP address is the source IP.  ","x":-280,"y":4509,"width":500,"height":190}
	],
	"edges":[
